  "live_records": {
    "q-183364": {
      "dom_id": "step-q183364",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n${2}^{n+2}>2n+3$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}>{{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$\n{{field-3}},\nwe have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}>{{field-5}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}>{{field-7}}\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${2}^{n+2}>2n+3$ for all $n\\ge 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{2}^{n+2}>2n+3\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{2}^{1+2}>2(1)+3\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+2}>2k+3\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{(k+1)+2} & >2(k+1)+3 \\\\ {2}^{k+3} & >2k+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives:\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+3}>2k+5.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${2}^{n+2}>2n+3$ for all $n\\ge 1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
