    "q-183369": {
      "dom_id": "step-q183369",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n${n}^{4}<{4}^{n}$ for all $n>4$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case {{field-1}}.\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}<{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$\n{{field-4}},\nwe have\n\n$$\n{{field-5}}<{{field-6}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}<{{field-8}}\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${n}^{4}<{4}^{n}$ for all $n>4.$",
      "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{4}<{4}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n>4,$ the base case is $P(5).$ So, we substitute $n=5$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=5.$\n\n$$\n{5}^{4}>{4}^{5}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k>4.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k>4,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}<{4}^{k}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^{4}<{4}^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${n}^{4}<{4}^{n}$ for all $n>4.$",
